@@ -0,0 +1,40 @@
+---
+id: task-004-implement-json-merge-patch-rfc-7386
+type: task
+title: "Implement JSON Merge Patch (RFC 7386)"
+status: approved
+---
+
+## Request
+
+A client sends partial updates in JSON Merge Patch format (RFC 7386), simpler than the already
+implemented JSON Patch (RFC 6902). Export `applyMergePatch(target, patch)` from `src/index.ts`,
+applying a merge patch to a target JSON document and returning the merged document.
+
+## What was done
+
+- Added `applyMergePatch(target: JsonValue, patch: JsonValue): JsonValue` to `src/index.ts`,
+  implementing the RFC 7386 section 2 algorithm directly: if `patch` is not a JSON object, it
+  replaces the target wholesale (at any recursion depth, including the top level); otherwise each
+  member of `patch` is merged into a clone of `target` (or a fresh object if `target` is not itself
+  an object), recursing for nested object values and deleting the target member when the patch value
+  is `null`.
+- Added a small `isJsonObject` helper (true for a non-null, non-array `object`) shared by the merge
+  logic, mirroring the existing `Array.isArray` / `typeof` checks already used elsewhere in the file.
+- Reused the existing `clone` and `setMember` helpers (from the RFC 6902 `applyPatch` work, see
+  dl-003) so `applyMergePatch` gets the same guarantees for free: the input `target` is never
+  mutated, and a member literally named `"__proto__"` is read and written as an ordinary own
+  property rather than being routed through the legacy `Object.prototype.__proto__` accessor.
+- Added tests to `src/index.test.ts`: an immutability/no-aliasing check, all of RFC 7386 appendix A's
+  worked examples (labelled as such), one additional edge case (removing a member absent from the
+  target is a no-op), and a `"__proto__"` member safety test matching the style of the existing
+  `applyPatch` ones. The `"__proto__"` test needed its patch object built via `JSON.parse` rather
+  than an object literal, since `{ __proto__: ... }` as a literal sets the literal's own prototype
+  instead of creating an own property — the literal form passed by accident (no own `"__proto__"` key
+  was ever produced) until the test was changed to parse JSON, which is also how the equivalent
+  `applyPatch` test already builds its fixture.
+- Verified with `npm run typecheck` and `npm test`: all 74 tests pass (18 new), no type errors.
+
+No decision-log entry was needed: RFC 7386's merge algorithm is unambiguous as written, and the two
+implementation choices with real latitude (immutability, `"__proto__"` write safety) just reuse the
+helpers and reasoning already recorded in dl-003 for `applyPatch`/`clone`.
